anaging patient expectations. [Data: Entities (110); Relationships (136)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Differentiation between Chronic and Episodic Migraine\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Medication Overuse Headache (MOH) is a recognized headache disorder that can co-occur with Chronic Migraine when a patient meets the diagnostic criteria for both conditions. This comorbidity is clinically significant because it indicates that excessive use of acute headache medications may contribute to the chronicity of migraine symptoms. The ICHD-3 acknowledges this relationship, allowing for a dual diagnosis when appropriate. This co-diagnosis has important implications for treatment, as managing MOH often requires discontinuing overused medications, which can be challenging but essential for improving outcomes. The presence of MOH in patients with Chronic Migraine underscores the complexity of managing chronic headache disorders. [Data: Entities (113); Relationships (137)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Co-diagnosis of Chronic Migraine and Medication Overuse Headache\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"New Daily Persistent Headache (NDPH) is a distinct headache disorder characterized by a continuous and unremitting headache that begins abruptly within 24 hours of onset. The ICHD-3 acknowledges that NDPH can share features with Chronic Migraine, leading to potential diagnostic overlap. This overlap necessitates careful differential diagnosis to avoid misclassification. The ICHD-3 provides guidance on distinguishing NDPH from Chronic Migraine based on onset pattern and symptom profile. This distinction is crucial for appropriate treatment, as the management strategies for these two conditions may differ significantly. [Data: Entities (111); Relationships (138)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Diagnostic overlap and differentiation between Chronic Migraine and New Daily Persistent Headache\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"expl